# Solution — GIAM 3.4 Problem 10

## Problem

In an earlier exercise we proved that the quadratic equation $ ax^2+bx+c=0 $ has two solutions if $ ac < 0 $. Find a counterexample which shows that this implication cannot be replaced with a biconditional.

## What Has to Be Refuted

The established theorem is

$$$
ac < 0 \implies ax^2+bx+c=0 \text{ has two solutions.}
$$$

Turning it into a biconditional would additionally assert the **converse**,

$$$
ax^2+bx+c=0 \text{ has two solutions} \implies ac < 0 ,
$$$

so a counterexample is a quadratic with two solutions and $ ac \ge 0 $.

## Answer

$$$
\boxed{\;x^2-3x+2 = 0 : \quad\text{roots } 1 \text{ and } 2, \quad\text{yet } ac = 1\cdot2 = 2 \not< 0 .\;}
$$$

## Verification

With $ a=1 $, $ b=-3 $, $ c=2 $:

$$$
x^2-3x+2 = (x-1)(x-2) ,
$$$

so the equation has the two distinct solutions $ x=1 $ and $ x=2 $. Substituting confirms both: $ 1-3+2=0 $ and $ 4-6+2=0 $. Meanwhile

$$$
ac = 1\cdot 2 = 2 > 0 ,
$$$

so the hypothesis $ ac<0 $ fails while the conclusion holds. The converse is therefore false, and the implication cannot be strengthened to a biconditional. $ \blacksquare $

An even more economical witness is $ x^2+x = x(x+1) $, with roots $ 0 $ and $ -1 $ and $ ac = 0 $ — though $ ac=0 $ sits exactly on the boundary, so $ x^2-3x+2 $ with $ ac $ strictly positive is the cleaner refutation.

## The Picture

Plotting $ b^2 $ against $ ac $ shows the whole situation at once: the region where two solutions exist is strictly larger than the region $ ac<0 $, and the gap between them is where the counterexamples live.

!(90%)[The b-squared versus ac plane, with the two-solution region and the ac-negative half-plane](problem-10-discriminant.svg "A diagram in five parts. The first states the true theorem — ac less than 0 implies the equation has two solutions — and the false converse — two solutions implies ac less than 0 — so the implication cannot be upgraded to a biconditional. The second gives the counterexample: x squared minus 3x plus 2 equals the product of x minus 1 and x minus 2, which has the two solutions x equals 1 and x equals 2, yet ac equals 1 times 2 equals 2, which is not negative. The centre is a plot with ac on the horizontal axis, running from minus 4 to 6, and b squared on the vertical axis, running from 0 to 14. A green line rises from the origin representing b squared equals 4ac. Everything above that line is shaded, marking the region where the quadratic has two real solutions. The left half, where ac is negative, is shaded darker: all of it lies above the line, because b squared is at least 0 which exceeds 4ac. The pale green wedge between the vertical axis and the line is the region where there are still two solutions but ac is at least 0, and every point there refutes the converse. The white region below the line is where there are no real solutions. Four points are marked: x squared minus 3x plus 2 at ac equals 2 and b squared equals 9, with roots 1 and 2, sitting in the pale wedge; x squared plus x at ac equals 0 and b squared equals 1, with roots 0 and minus 1; x squared minus 1 at ac equals minus 1 and b squared equals 0, with roots plus and minus 1, in the dark region; and x squared plus x plus 1 at ac equals 1 and b squared equals 1, with no real roots, below the line. A band below explains what ac less than 0 actually means: the product of the two roots is c over a, so ac less than 0 says exactly that the roots have opposite signs, verified over every a, b and c from minus 8 to 8. That is a real restriction, since 1 and 2 are both positive. Geometrically, ac less than 0 means the parabola's value at x equals 0 has the opposite sign to its arms, so it must cross the axis once on each side of the origin; a parabola crossing twice on the same side is invisible to the test. A final band reports how much the test misses: among all integer triples with a nonzero and a, b, c between minus 8 and 8, exactly 2832 give two distinct real roots, of which 2176 satisfy ac less than 0 and 656 do not, so the sufficient condition catches about 77 percent of them. The correct biconditional replaces ac less than 0 by the discriminant: two solutions if and only if b squared minus 4ac is greater than 0. The sign test ac less than 0 survives as a quick check needing no arithmetic, while the discriminant is exact but must be computed.")

The counterexample is the marked point at $ ac=2 $, $ b^2=9 $: comfortably above the line, so two solutions, but on the wrong side of the vertical axis for the sign test to notice.

## Remark — Why It Only Goes One Way

A quadratic with $ a\ne0 $ has two distinct real solutions exactly when its discriminant is positive:

$$$
\Delta = b^2-4ac > 0 .
$$$

If $ ac<0 $ then $ -4ac > 0 $, and since $ b^2 \ge 0 $ always,

$$$
\Delta = b^2 - 4ac \ge 0 + (\text{something positive}) > 0 .
$$$

So $ ac<0 $ is genuinely sufficient — confirmed on every integer triple with $ |a|,|b|,|c|\le 8 $ and $ a\ne0 $.

But the implication runs only one way, because $ b^2 $ can supply the positivity on its own. Whenever

$$$
0 \le 4ac < b^2 ,
$$$

the discriminant is positive while $ ac $ is not negative. The counterexample sits squarely in that window: $ 4ac = 8 $ and $ b^2 = 9 $.

## Remark — What $ ac<0 $ Really Says

The condition has a clean meaning that explains both its usefulness and its limits. For a quadratic with roots $ r_1, r_2 $, Vieta's formulas give

$$$
r_1 r_2 = \frac{c}{a} ,
$$$

so $ ac<0 $ — equivalently $ c/a < 0 $ — says precisely that **the two roots have opposite signs**. This was verified over every integer triple in $ [-8,8]^3 $ with $ a\ne0 $.

That is a real restriction, and it is exactly what the counterexample violates: $ 1 $ and $ 2 $ are both positive, so their product is positive, so $ ac>0 $ — even though there are certainly two of them.

Geometrically, $ c = f(0) $ and $ a $ controls which way the parabola opens. When $ ac<0 $ the value at the origin has the opposite sign to the arms, so the curve is forced across the axis once on each side of $ 0 $. A parabola that crosses twice on the *same* side of the origin — as $ (x-1)(x-2) $ does — has two roots that the sign test simply cannot see.

## Remark — How Much the Test Misses

The condition is not a near-miss. Among all integer triples with $ a\ne0 $ and $ -8\le a,b,c\le 8 $:

$$$
\begin{array}{l|r}
\text{triples with two distinct real roots} & 2832 \\
\quad\text{of which } ac<0 & 2176 \\
\quad\text{of which } ac\ge 0 & \textbf{656}
\end{array}
$$$

So roughly one case in four is a counterexample to the converse. Finding one by random search would have taken a handful of attempts; the difficulty of this exercise is not locating a witness but noticing that the converse is a separate claim at all.

## Remark — The Correct Biconditional

A false converse is most useful when it points at the right statement. Replacing $ ac<0 $ with the discriminant gives a genuine biconditional:

$$$
ax^2+bx+c=0 \;\text{ has two distinct real solutions} \iff b^2-4ac > 0 \qquad (a\ne0) .
$$$

The three conditions line up as a strict hierarchy:

$$$
\begin{array}{l|l|l}
\textbf{condition} & \textbf{gives} & \textbf{cost to check} \\ \hline
ac < 0 & \text{two solutions (one way only)} & \text{compare two signs} \\
b^2-4ac > 0 & \text{two solutions (both ways)} & \text{three multiplications} \\
b^2-4ac = 0 & \text{one repeated solution} & \text{--} \\
b^2-4ac < 0 & \text{no real solutions} & \text{--}
\end{array}
$$$

This is why the weaker statement was worth proving in the first place. $ ac<0 $ can be read off by eye — do $ a $ and $ c $ have opposite signs? — with no arithmetic at all, and when it holds you know immediately that there are two roots straddling the origin. The discriminant is exact but demands computation. A sufficient condition that is cheap to check is often more useful in practice than a necessary and sufficient one that is not; the error lies only in mistaking the first for the second.

## Remark — The Shape of the Mistake

This exercise is a specific instance of the most common error in reading mathematical statements. Given a proved implication $ P \Rightarrow Q $, there are four related conditionals, and only two of them are guaranteed:

$$$
\begin{array}{l|l|l}
\textbf{name} & \textbf{statement} & \textbf{status here} \\ \hline
\text{original} & ac<0 \Rightarrow \text{two solutions} & \text{true (proved earlier)} \\
\text{contrapositive} & \text{no two solutions} \Rightarrow ac\ge0 & \text{true --- equivalent to it} \\
\text{converse} & \text{two solutions} \Rightarrow ac<0 & \textbf{false} \\
\text{inverse} & ac\ge0 \Rightarrow \text{no two solutions} & \textbf{false}
\end{array}
$$$

The converse and inverse are equivalent to each other and independent of the original, so refuting one refutes both — the single example $ x^2-3x+2 $ kills the bottom two rows at once. “Cannot be replaced with a biconditional” is exactly the statement that the third row fails, and $ (x-1)(x-2) $ is all the argument required.
